  "status_text": "What if you cross out a row of a grid and the wiring barely gets any cheaper? A contributor has pinned down what happens if that is true for every row. Take at least three rows, where for any two rows, each has a connection the other lacks. For a circuit on one-row, one-column inputs using at most s fan-in-two AND/OR gates, either some row can be dropped with a saving of at least two from that budget, or a circuit within budget reads each row input just once, in a two-input OR, with all gates reading earlier wires (L-meta-25491, checked).",
